PR #73  overall: potential_risk
Spec: SPMP spec clarifies TOR entries require spmpaddr[i-1] < spmpaddr[i] with entry 0 defaulting lower bound to zero, registers use SXLEN not MXLEN, and locked entries cannot be bypassed via miselect — preventing undefined memory protection gaps exploitable by less-privileged modes.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/head.S:237-240  _start_kernel
      This configures an M-mode PMP (not SPMP) entry in NAPOT mode covering the full address space (addr=-1). It uses PMP_A_NAPOT, not TOR, so the TOR ordering invariant does not apply. This is only compiled under CONFIG_RISCV_M_MODE and provides an identity mapping so Linux can boot on M-mode-only hardware. It does not touch SPMP CSRs at all.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:427-428  (CSR definitions)
      Only M-mode PMP CSR addresses are defined (CSR_PMPCFG0 at 0x3a0, CSR_PMPADDR0 at 0x3b0). No S-mode SPMP CSR definitions (spmpcfg, spmpaddr) exist anywhere in the tree. If hardware implements SPMP and firmware leaves SPMP entries in an active TOR configuration, the kernel has no mechanism to discover, validate, or reprogram them. This is a latent risk: the kernel implicitly assumes SPMP is either absent or pre-configured permissively by firmware, but never checks.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kernel/cpufeature.c:579-579  (riscv_isa_ext_data array)
      This registers the Smmpm extension (Machine-mode Pointer Masking), not SPMP (S-mode Physical Memory Protection). Smmpm and SPMP are distinct extensions. The kernel correctly treats Smmpm as an informational ISA feature with no SPMP implications. No violation.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:469-470  (VTYPE definitions)
      __riscv_xlen is used only for vector CSR layout (VTYPE_VILL_SHIFT) and interrupt cause flag (CAUSE_IRQ_FLAG), not for SPMP CSR width derivation. Since Linux always runs in S-mode, __riscv_xlen reflects SXLEN. No SPMP-specific register-width mismatch between MXLEN and SXLEN can occur here because no SPMP CSRs are accessed at all.

